# Decode silicon for DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash

**A modeled hardware-architecture study of [deepseek-ultrafast](https://github.com/basetenlabs/deepseek-ultrafast).
It shows where the time goes, what a custom accelerator gives, and how much of that gain software can give first.**
All MDA-1 and projected-GPU numbers are **simulated/projected**. We quote the numbers marked *measured* from the deepseek-ultrafast README.

> **Status:** This is a performance-model study with one verified RTL block. There is no silicon, no synthesis and no GPU profile.
> We revised the claims after an adversarial review ([what changed](docs/05_limitations.md#revisions-after-adversarial-review)).

## Abstract

deepseek-ultrafast serves DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash one request at a time. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash is a
552B-parameter MoE with FP4 experts. The engine gets {{ K.meas4.decode_tok_s|n }} tok/s on 4 x GB300 and {{ K.meas8.decode_tok_s|n }} tok/s on 8 x B300 (*measured*).
We rebuilt the workload from the Rust/CUDA source of the engine and the checkpoint headers of the model. Then
we built an op-level performance simulator and calibrated it to the two reported step times.

1. **About half of each decode step is fixed latency, not weight streaming.** The calibrated model
   attributes {{ K.fixed4_ms|n(2) }} ms of the 3.30 ms step ({{ K.fixed4_share|pct }}) to dependent-stage
   latency and collective latency. The range is {{ K.fixed_lo|pct }}-{{ K.fixed_hi|pct }} across all alternative calibrations.
   The average HBM rate is {{ pct(W.decode_per_gpu_tp4.total/1e9/K.meas4.step_ms/8.0) }} of peak.
2. **The fix is an execution model, not FLOPs.** The execution model replaces dependent kernel launches with one
   persistent program in a static order. Each stage hands off to the next stage with flags. The program streams the
   dense weights of the next layers while the experts of the current layer are in transit. On the **same 4 x GB300**
   the model projects **{{ C.gpu_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user ({{ K.sw_vs_meas|x }} the measured engine)**. The
   gain has three parts:
   - The persistent program gives +{{ ((K.abl_by.A2.tok_s_user/K.abl_by.A1.tok_s_user-1)*100)|n(0) }}% if a stage hand-off can decrease to ~1 µs. This is the least certain step.
   - Flags give +{{ ((K.abl_by.A3.tok_s_user/K.abl_by.A2.tok_s_user-1)*100)|n(0) }}%.
   - Cross-layer weight prefetch gives +{{ ((K.abl_by.A4.tok_s_user/K.abl_by.A3.tok_s_user-1)*100)|n(0) }}%.
3. **Custom silicon that uses that execution model gets the same speed. It is not faster.** MDA-1 is a
   decode ASIC. It has 2 dies, 8 x HBM3E at the 8 TB/s of GB300, 2 PF FP8, a hardware MoE router (RTL-verified)
   and in-fabric all-reduce. The model projects **{{ C.asic.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user**, {{ pct(C.asic.tok_s_user / K.roofline) }} of the HBM roofline of MDA-1.

   We draw all assumptions of MDA-1 jointly. Then the result is {{ K.MC.vs_gpu_measured.p5|x }}-{{ K.MC.vs_gpu_measured.p95|x }} the measured engine (5th-95th percentile). It is
   {{ K.MC.vs_sw.p5|x(2) }}-{{ K.MC.vs_sw.p95|x(2) }} the same-bandwidth GPU software (median {{ K.MC.vs_sw.p50|x(2) }}).

   **The case for MDA-1 is economic.** MDA-1 gets
   {{ K.usd_vs_sw|x }} lower $/M tokens and {{ K.tpj_vs_sw|x }} tokens/J against GPUs that run the same software.
   These GPUs are owned at $45K and draw 60% of TDP. Across $25-65K per GPU and 40-100% of TDP, those ratios are
   {{ K.usd_vs_sw_lo|x }}-{{ K.usd_vs_sw_hi|x }} and {{ K.tpj_vs_sw_lo|x }}-{{ K.tpj_vs_sw_hi|x }}. Break-even is about {{ K.be_sw_300|n(-2) }} chips at $300M NRE.
4. **Bandwidth alone does not fix it.** Rubin has 19.2 TB/s ({{ K.rubin_bw_ratio|n(1) }}x GB300). With the per-stage
   latency of today, the model projects only +{{ (K.rubin_vs_gb300*100)|n(0) }}% for Rubin over GB300. With dataflow-style software on both,
   the bandwidth of Rubin gives {{ K.rubin_df_vs_df|x(2) }} ({{ C.rubin_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} vs {{ C.gpu_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user).

